WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
Euler’s method is simple but not very accurate.

2
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:11.712
In this part we look at methods that sample the slope at several points and average: the improved Euler or Heun method,

3
00:00:11.712 --> 00:00:14.928
and the classical Runge–Kutta method.

4
00:00:14.928 --> 00:00:19.022
Then we see why a large step can be dangerous.

5
00:00:19.272 --> 00:00:22.872
In Heun’s method we first predict with Euler,

6
00:00:22.872 --> 00:00:28.224
then compute the slope at the end of the step and step with the average of the two slopes.

7
00:00:28.224 --> 00:00:36.240
RK4 takes four slopes: at the start, twice at the midpoint and at the end, and averages them with weights one,

8
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.302
two, two, one.

9
00:00:39.552 --> 00:00:46.632
Again y prime equals x plus y, y of zero equals one, h equals zero point one.

10
00:00:46.632 --> 00:00:50.328
In RK4, k one equals one.

11
00:00:50.328 --> 00:00:56.880
k two is at the midpoint: x is zero point zero five and y is one point zero five,

12
00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.398
so k two equals one point one.

13
00:01:00.648 --> 00:01:06.696
k three is also at the midpoint but uses k two: one point one zero five.

14
00:01:06.696 --> 00:01:11.760
k four is at the end of the step: one point two one zero five.

15
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:19.886
Averaging gives y one about one point one one zero three four two, with an error of about two ten-millionths.

16
00:01:20.136 --> 00:01:24.600
Compare the three methods at x equal to zero point five.

17
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:31.560
When the step is halved the Euler error drops by two, Heun’s by four and RK4’s by sixteen.

18
00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:35.582
So the orders are one, two and four.

19
00:01:35.832 --> 00:01:37.920
Now stability.

20
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:45.384
For y prime equals minus lambda y, Euler multiplies y by one minus h lambda at every step.

21
00:01:45.384 --> 00:01:50.880
If h is larger than two over lambda, this factor exceeds one in size,

22
00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:58.032
and the numerical solution grows with alternating sign, although the exact solution on the graph decays.

23
00:01:58.032 --> 00:02:02.438
SciPy’s solve ivp chooses the step automatically.

24
00:02:02.688 --> 00:02:09.000
Question: for which step is explicit Euler stable on y prime equals minus twenty y?

25
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.048
Pause the video, choose your answer,

26
00:02:12.048 --> 00:02:17.726
then read the explanation and go back to the relevant part of the lesson if needed.
